Item 05 is done. Everything is written, tested and verified; nothing was committed and nothing touched `solutions/`.

**Answer to §2.9: no, the matrix is not part of the hardness story.** With customer labels fixed, re-covering the same edge set with different cliques leaves the default node count to refute `optimum − 1` exactly unchanged in every one of 15,900 pairs. Graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set, so the kill criterion is met.

What was measured, all at 10 ≤ n ≤ 40:

- **Re-coverings.** 1,400 campaign bases, 15,900 applicable re-coverings by split, merge and greedy edge-clique cover, in 12,031 matrix classes. Every one kept the base's neighbour masks and nauty certificate, solved independently to the same optimum, and matched the base's default node count exactly.
- **The one matrix channel.** Under `csearch`, 394 pairs differ, all among the 622 where the re-covering crossed the `better_move` density threshold. Theorem 2 saves 10–13% of nodes at the median for n ≥ 25, up to 25%, and never costs a node. That is a configuration rule, not a property of the problem.
- **Labelling matters more than the matrix.** Renaming customers of the same matrix changes the default count on 588 of 800 bases at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median and up to 2.3×, up to 8× under `csearch`. This is the floor for any graph-invariant predictor.
- **§8's counts.** `count_search` is equal in all 4,516 re-covered pairs at n ≤ 15. `count_closing` differs in nearly every greedy pair, by 2.9–4.5× at the median and up to 1,540×.
- **Corpus pairs are uninformative.** The 150 graph classes with several matrix classes are near-complete graphs refuted in 0–13 nodes, and the report says so.
- **Kill test.** Graph-only MAE 0.0948 against full 0.0968 in log10 nodes on the deduplicated campaign, matrix-only 25–30% worse, grouped by graph class.

